'''José Manuel Calderón Borrallo''' ({{IPA|es|xoˈse kaldeˈɾon}}; born September 28, 1981) is a Spanish [[basketball]] executive and former player who is a special advisor for the [[Cleveland Cavaliers]] of the [[National Basketball Association]] (NBA). He played professionally for 21 years including 14 seasons in the NBA, primarily with the [[Toronto Raptors]]
with whom he set an NBA record for the highest free throw percentage in a season (98.1% in 2008-09). With the [[Spain national basketball team|Spain national team]], he won a [[FIBA Basketball World Cup|FIBA World Cup]] title in [[2006 FIBA World Championship|2006]], two [[Basketball at the Summer Olympics|Olympic]] silver medals in [[Basketball at the 2008 Summer Olympics – Men's tournament|2008]] and [[Basketball at the 2012 Summer Olympics – Men's tournament|2012]], as well as a bronze medal at the [[Basketball at the 2016 Summer Olympics – Men's tournament|2016 Summer Olympics]]. He also won a [[EuroBasket]] title in [[EuroBasket 2011|2011]], two silver medals in [[EuroBasket 2003|2003]] and [[EuroBasket 2007|2007]] as well as a bronze in [[EuroBasket 2013|2013]]. Calderón earned an [[EuroBasket All-Tournament Team|All-EuroBasket Team]] selection in [[EuroBasket 2007|2007]].

==Early life and European career==
José Calderón was born and raised in the town of [[Villanueva de la Serena]], in Spain.<ref name=bio>[http://www.josemanuelcalderon.com/#bio José Manuel Calderón – About Me]</ref> His main inspiration for basketball came from his father, who used to play basketball for his hometown's team, Doncel La Serena.<ref name="bio" /> Calderón received an offer to play professional basketball after he won a junior team championship.<ref name="bio" />

After playing for Diputación Foral Alava, Calderón joined [[CB Lucentum Alicante|Lucentum Alicante]] ([[Liga Española de Baloncesto|Spanish Second Division]]) in 1999, and he led his team to the [[Liga ACB|ACB]] ([[Spanish basketball league system|Spanish First Division]]).<ref name="bio" /> The team had to return to the second division because of fierce competition.<ref name="bio" /> This helped Calderón adapt to the competition, and catalyzed an improvement to his game.<ref name="bio" />

In 2001, Calderón joined ACB team [[Baloncesto Fuenlabrada|Fuenlabrada]].<ref name="bio" /> He averaged 9.7 points, and 1.7 assists in 18.6 minutes per game, and shot 83.2% from the free throw line.<ref>[http://www.josemanuelcalderon.com/#pro José Manuel Calderón – Player Profile]</ref> By the end of the season, he joined the Spain national team.<ref name="bio" /> In 2002, he played for the senior [[Spain national basketball team|Spain national team]] in the [[2002 FIBA World Championship]] at Indianapolis, where his team ended up in fifth place.<ref name="bio" />

After the summer, Calderón signed with [[Saski Baskonia|TAU Vitoria]].<ref name="bio" /> Calderon played three seasons with Tau Vitoria.<ref name="bio" /> His team won the [[Spanish Basketball King's Cup]], and were also runners-up in both the Liga ACB (Spanish League) and the [[EuroLeague]].<ref name="bio" /> In 2002–03, he averaged 7.5 points, 2.4 rebounds, 1.4 assists and 1.1 steals in the EuroLeague. In 2003–04, he averaged 7.2 points, 1.4 rebounds, 2.0 assists, and 1.1 steals in the EuroLeague.  In 2004–05, he averaged 11.6 points, 3.4 rebounds, 2.6 assists and 1.5 steals in the EuroLeague.<ref>[http://www.euroleague.net/competition/players/showplayer?pcode=BDG Calderon, Jose Manuel – Euroleague.net]</ref>

==NBA career==
[[Image:Josè Calderon.JPG|left|thumb|upright=0.65|Calderón played mostly off the bench in his rookie season (2005–06) with the Raptors.]]

===Toronto Raptors (2005–2013)===
====Rookie season====
Calderón entered the NBA when former Raptors GM [[Rob Babcock]] persuaded the Spanish guard to sign with the [[Toronto Raptors]] on August 3, 2005.<ref>[http://www.nba.com/raptors/news/PR_calderon_050803.html "Raptors Sign Jose Calderon"], nba.com/raptors, August 3, 2005, Retrieved June 21, 2007.</ref> Known as a talented playmaker, he struggled with his shooting in his first year in the NBA. By the end of the [[2005–06 NBA season|2005–06 season]], he was ranked third among all rookies in [[assist (basketball)|assists]] with 4.5 per game.<ref name="nbabio">{{cite web|title=Jose Calderon stats, details, videos and news|url=http://www.nba.com/players/jose/calderon/101181|website=NBA.com|access-date=December 4, 2017}}</ref> He finished the season with 64 played games, having started in 11 of them.<ref name=nbabio/> He averaged 5.5 [[point (basketball)|points]], 4.5 assists, 2.2 [[rebound (basketball)|rebounds]] and an assists-per-turnover ratio of 2.85.<ref name=nbabio/>

====2006–07 season====
Throughout the [[2006–07 NBA season|2006–07 season]], Calderón played primarily as backup to [[T. J. Ford]], turning in solid numbers from the bench.<ref>Smith, Doug, [https://www.thestar.com/Sports/article/258727 "Calderon makes point to remember team first"], thestar.com, September 20, 2007, Retrieved September 21, 2007.</ref> As acknowledged by coach [[Sam Mitchell (basketball)|Sam Mitchell]] as well as his teammates, Calderón's form was instrumental to the Raptors' push for a [[NBA Playoffs|playoff spot]], and later, the [[Atlantic Division (NBA)|Atlantic Division]] title.<ref>[http://www.nba.com/raptors/news/quotes_013107.html Post-Game Quotes], nba.com/raptors, January 31, 2007, Retrieved June 21, 2007.</ref> He ended his regular season with improved averages of 8.8 points and 5.1 assists per game and .525 in [[field goal percentage]], even though he had less playing time compared to his rookie season.<ref name=nbabio/> In the [[2007 NBA Playoffs]], Calderón played in all of Toronto's games in the series against the [[New Jersey Nets]], but the Raptors were defeated 4–2.<ref>[http://www.nba.com/raptors/news/postup_050407.html Raptors Post Up], nba.com/raptors, May 4, 2007, Retrieved June 21, 2007.</ref> Calderón averaged 13.0 points and 5.3 assists per game in his first NBA playoff season.<ref name=nbabio/>
[[Image:jose calderon zz.jpg|thumb|Calderón taking a [[Jump shot (basketball)|jump shot]] in a {{nowrap|2006–07}} game against the [[Washington Wizards]]]]

====2007–08 season====
Toronto continued to deploy Ford and Calderón in tandem for the point guard position to good effect. Ford was injured for several games in November and December and the Spaniard stepped in with impressive performances.<ref>[http://www.nba.com/raptors/news/recap_121207.html Raptors Cruise Past Mavericks For Third Straight Win], nba.com/raptors, December 12, 2007, Retrieved December 13, 2007.</ref> In three games against the [[Memphis Grizzlies]], [[Cleveland Cavaliers]] and [[Chicago Bulls]], Calderón provided 37 assists while committing only three [[turnover (basketball)|turnovers]].<ref>Smith, Doug, [https://www.thestar.com/Sports/article/279755 "Calderon leads Raptors past Bulls"], thestar.com, November 26, 2007, Retrieved November 26, 2007.</ref> 14 games into the season, he was also leading the league in assist-to-turnover ratio with 5.67 to 1,<ref>[http://www.nba.com/statistics/player/Turnovers.jsp?league=00&season=22007&conf=OVERALL&position=0&splitType=9&qualified=Y&yearsExp=-1&sortOrder=4&splitDD=All%20Teams 2007–08 TURNOVERS] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121106170915/http://www.nba.com/statistics/player/Turnovers.jsp?league=00&season=22007&conf=OVERALL&position=0&splitType=9&qualified=Y&yearsExp=-1&sortOrder=4&splitDD=All%20Teams |date=November 6, 2012 }}, nba.com, accessed May 2, 2008.</ref> but even so, he said: "But how I feel about the team right now is that we need T.J. Ford [for the next game]."<ref>Grange, Michael, [https://www.theglobeandmail.com/servlet/story/RTGAM.20071125.wspt-raptors25/GSStory/GlobeSportsBasketball/Raptors "Toronto goes to the well, finds it's deep"], globeandmail.com, November 25, 2007, Retrieved November 26, 2007.</ref> 35 games into the season, the Spaniard increased the turnover ratio to 6.15 to 1, setting him on course to break an NBA record (he went to end the season as the league-leader with 5.38 to 1).<ref>Ewing, Lori, [https://www.thestar.com/Sports/article/292924 "Humble Calderon on pace for efficiency record"], thestar.com, January 10, 2008, Retrieved January 11, 2008.</ref><ref name=200708stats>[http://www.nba.com/statistics/player/Assists.jsp?league=00&season=22007&conf=OVERALL&position=0&splitType=9&splitScope=GAME&qualified=Y&yearsExp=-1&splitDD= 2007–08 ASSISTS LEADERS :  Assists Per Game] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080501193840/http://www.nba.com/statistics/player/Assists.jsp?league=00&season=22007&conf=OVERALL&position=0&splitType=9&splitScope=GAME&qualified=Y&yearsExp=-1&splitDD= |date=May 1, 2008 }}, nba.com, accessed May 2, 2008.</ref> He shot at least 50% on field goals, 40% on three-pointers, and 90% on free-throws, and is sometimes listed as a member of the [[50-40-90 Club (NBA)|NBA's 50–40–90 Club]], even though he was sixteen free-throws shy of attaining the [https://web.archive.org/web/20101117041423/http://www.nba.com/leader_requirements.html NBA league minimum] number of makes in this category. By mid-season, he was touted as a possibility for making the All-Star reserve team.<ref>Smith, Doug, [https://www.thestar.com/Sports/article/297839 "Calderon playing like all-star"], thestar.com, January 27, 2008, Retrieved January 28, 2008.</ref> Even after Ford returned from injury for the final third of the campaign, Calderón was able to keep his place in the starting lineup. Ford had initially agreed to playing backup, but reportedly became frustrated at not being able to start.<ref name=ford>Feschuk, Dave, [https://www.thestar.com/Sports/NBA/article/404839 "Raptors' Ford may be wearing out his welcome"], thestar.com, March 28, 2008, Retrieved March 29, 2008.</ref> Calderón eventually asked the coaching staff to play Ford as starter instead, leading to widespread praise from the coaching staff, teammates, and fans. Given that he was going to become a free agent by the end of the campaign, there were even calls for Ford to be traded so that the team could be built around Calderón as point guard.<ref name=ford/> By the end of the regular season, Calderón was fifth in the league for assists per game,<ref name=200708stats/> and in the [[2008 NBA Playoffs]], Ford split the playing time with him about equally, with the two players combining for averages of 23.4 points and 13.6 assists per game.<ref name=nbabio/><ref>[http://www.nba.com/playerfile/tj_ford/career_stats.html T.J. Ford Info Page – Career Stats and Totals] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070311010119/http://www.nba.com/playerfile/tj_ford/career_stats.html |date=March 11, 2007 }}, nba.com, accessed September 8, 2007.</ref> However, the Raptors were eliminated by the [[Orlando Magic]] in the first round four games to one.<ref>[http://www.nba.com/games/20080428/TORORL/recap.html Magic Beat Raptors, Move to Second Round] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090308170704/http://www.nba.com/games/20080428/TORORL/recap.html |date=March 8, 2009 }}, nba.com, April 29, 2008, accessed May 1, 2008.</ref>

====2008–09 season====
On July 9, 2008, Calderón re-signed with the Raptors to a multi-year deal, reported to be worth $7.5 million to $8.5 million a season.<ref>[http://www.canada.com/topics/sports/story.html?id=6e3b34a9-485a-4f1f-a8c5-78efd0cbcd20 Raptors sign Calderon to long-term deal] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121109171612/http://www.canada.com/topics/sports/story.html?id=6e3b34a9-485a-4f1f-a8c5-78efd0cbcd20 |date=November 9, 2012 }}, canada.com, July 9, 2009, Retrieved January 22, 2010.</ref><ref>[http://www.nba.com/raptors/news/calderon_070908.html Raptors Re-Sign Calderon], nba.com/raptors, July 9, 2008, Retrieved July 28, 2008.</ref> With the departure of Ford to the [[Indiana Pacers]], the Spaniard was slated to start the point guard position for the Raptors. At the same time, new acquisition [[Jermaine O'Neal]] formed the frontcourt with Bosh. However, 17 games into the season, Toronto fired head coach [[Sam Mitchell (basketball)|Sam Mitchell]] and the line-ups were constantly shuffled. By the end of 2008, the Raptors were 12–20, but with Calderón out for almost a month thereafter, the team slipped to 16–28. When Calderón returned to the Raptors lineup, he helped snap a seven-game losing streak with a 114–94 victory over Chicago, recording 23 points on 9-of-10 shooting and 10 assists. Not long after, he set the second-longest streak in NBA history for consecutive free throws made (87).<ref>[http://www.nba.com/games/20090130/MILTOR/recap.html Bogut returns as Bucks beat Raptors 96–85], nba.com, January 30, 2009, accessed February 3, 2009.</ref> On March 13, 2009, Calderón surpassed [[Alvin Williams]]' franchise record for career assists with 1795.<ref>[http://www.nba.com/games/20090313/DETTOR/recap.html Hamilton, Pistons beat Raptors 99–95 in overtime] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120204052220/http://www.nba.com/games/20090313/DETTOR/recap.html |date=February 4, 2012 }}, nba.com, March 13, 2009, Retrieved March 14, 2009.</ref> On March 29, 2009, in a game against Chicago, he tied the franchise record for most assists in game with 19,<ref>[http://www.nba.com/games/20090329/CHITOR/recap.html Bosh, Bargnani lead Raptors to OT win over Bulls], nba.com, March 29, 2009, Retrieved March 30, 2009.</ref> and not long after, set an NBA record for highest free-throw percentage in a season, making 98.1% of his free throws in the season, missing only 3 of the 154 he took, overtaking [[Calvin Murphy]]’s record of 95.8% set in [[1980–81 NBA season|1981]]. These records, however, were overshadowed by the Raptors rapidly falling out of the playoffs picture soon after the All-Star break, and the criticism Calderón faced for being unable to defend and play an up-tempo game. Toronto eventually finished with a 33–49 record,<ref>[https://www.basketball-reference.com/leagues/NBA_2009.html 2008–09 NBA Season Summary], basketball-reference.com, Retrieved April 16, 2009.</ref> with the Spaniard leading the [[Eastern Conference (NBA)|Eastern Conference]] in assists per game and the league in free throw percentage and assist-to-turnover ratio.<ref>[http://www.nba.com/statistics/player/Assists.jsp?league=00&season=22008&conf=OVERALL&position=0&splitType=9&splitScope=GAME&qualified=Y&yearsExp=-1&splitDD= 2008–09 ASSISTS LEADERS :  Assists Per Game] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090428213559/http://www.nba.com/statistics/player/Assists.jsp?league=00&season=22008&conf=OVERALL&position=0&splitType=9&splitScope=GAME&qualified=Y&yearsExp=-1&splitDD= |date=April 28, 2009 }}, nba.com, Retrieved April 20, 2009.</ref><ref>[http://www.nba.com/statistics/player/FreeTS.jsp?league=00&season=22008&conf=OVERALL&position=0&splitType=9&splitScope=GAME&qualified=Y&yearsExp=-1&splitDD= 2008–09  FREE THROWS LEADERS:  Free Throw Percentage] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090428213319/http://www.nba.com/statistics/player/FreeTS.jsp?league=00&season=22008&conf=OVERALL&position=0&splitType=9&splitScope=GAME&qualified=Y&yearsExp=-1&splitDD= |date=April 28, 2009 }}, nba.com, Retrieved April 20, 2009.</ref>

====2009–10 season====
[[File:Jose calderon toronto raptors 2009 (cropped).JPG|thumb|upright|Going for a layup against the Orlando Magic in 2009]]
The Raptors underwent a significant roster overhaul in the preseason, such as bringing [[Jarrett Jack]] from Indiana to play as Calderón's back-up and [[point forward]] [[Hedo Türkoğlu]] to share in ball-handling duties. With a third of the season gone, the Spaniard picked up an injury that put him out for 12 games, resulting in Jack holding on to the starting spot even upon the former's return, paralleling the Ford-Calderón scenario in 2007–08. Still, the pair combined well and helped bring the Raptors to a 21–20 record at the mid-season mark.

During the early stages of the 2010 offseason, the Raptors had a deal in place to send Calderon and [[Reggie Evans]] to the [[Charlotte Bobcats]] for [[Tyson Chandler]] and [[Boris Diaw]]. However, [[Michael Jordan]], the owner of the Bobcats, nixed the deal right before it was finalized, which allowed Calderon to remain with the Raptors.

====2010–11 season====
Calderón was ranked fifth in assists per game (8.9) at the conclusion of the regular season.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.espn.com/nba/stats?season=2011|title = 2010-11 NBA Stat Leaders}}</ref>

====2011–12 season====
On February 12, 2012, Calderón scored a career high 30 points in a 94–92 loss against the Lakers.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.nba.com/games/20120212/LALTOR/gameinfo.html |title=Archived copy |website=[[NBA.com]] |access-date=February 15, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170109212921/http://www.nba.com/games/20120212/LALTOR/gameinfo.html |archive-date=January 9, 2017 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Calderón led the league in assists per turnover (4.5) for the third time in five years at the conclusion of the regular season.

===Detroit Pistons (2013)===
[[File:José Calderón Pistons.jpg|thumb|upright|Calderón with the Pistons in 2013]]
On January 30, 2013, Calderón was traded to the [[Detroit Pistons]] in a three-team trade which sent [[Tayshaun Prince]], [[Austin Daye]], and [[Ed Davis (basketball)|Ed Davis]] to the [[Memphis Grizzlies]] and [[Rudy Gay]] and [[Hamed Haddadi]] to Toronto.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.nba.com/pistons/news/detroit-pistons-acquire-guard-jose-calderon-three-team-trade|title=Detroit Pistons Acquire Guard Jose Calderon In Three-Team Trade|work=NBA.com|publisher=Turner Sports Interactive, Inc.|date=January 30, 2013|access-date=June 23, 2013}}</ref> He became the starting point guard for the Pistons, and in just his fourth game for the franchise, he recorded 23 points and 10 assists in the 105–100 win over the [[Milwaukee Bucks]].

Calderón finished the season averaging 11.3 points and 7.1 assists per game. He also led the league in three-point shooting percentage (.461) and was second in assists per turnover (4.1).

===Dallas Mavericks (2013–2014)===
On July 11, 2013, Calderón signed with the [[Dallas Mavericks]] to a reported four-year, $28 million contract.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.nba.com/mavericks/news/jose-calderon-signs-mavs|title=Jose Calderon Signs with the Mavs|work=NBA.com|publisher=Turner Sports Interactive, Inc.|date=July 11, 2013|access-date=July 14, 2013}}</ref> He became the Mavericks lead point guard, starting in 81 games. He had his best game on December 20, 2013, against his former team, the Toronto Raptors, where he recorded 23 points and 9 assists. Calderón finished the season averaging 11.4 points and 4.7 assists per game.

===New York Knicks (2014–2016)===
On June 25, 2014, Calderón, along with [[Shane Larkin]], [[Wayne Ellington]], [[Samuel Dalembert]] and two 2014 second-round picks, was traded to the [[New York Knicks]] in exchange for [[Tyson Chandler]] and [[Raymond Felton]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nba.com/knicks/knicks-acquire|title=Knicks Acquire Calderon, Three Others in Dallas Trade|date=June 25, 2014|work=NBA.com|access-date=June 25, 2014}}</ref> In the process, he re-joined former Raptor teammate [[Andrea Bargnani]]. After a preseason injury kept him on sidelines for the first 13 games of the season, he made his Knicks debut on November 22, 2014, as he recorded 3 points, 3 rebounds, 3 assists and 2 steals in the 91–83 win over the [[Philadelphia 76ers]].

On March 23, 2015, Calderón was ruled out for the rest of the season after undergoing a procedure on his strained left Achilles tendon.<ref>[http://www.sportando.com/en/usa/nba/156279/knicks-jose-calderon-underwent-surgery-done-for-the-season.html Knicks' Jose Calderon underwent surgery, done for the season]</ref>

On March 13, 2016, he made a game-winning three-point shot with 0.2 seconds left on the clock to defeat the [[Los Angeles Lakers]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.nba.com/games/20160313/NYKLAL/gameinfo.html |title=Calderon's buzzer-beating 3 lifts Knicks past Lakers, 90-87 |website=[[NBA.com]] |access-date=March 15, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160314005536/http://www.nba.com/games/20160313/NYKLAL/gameinfo.html |archive-date=March 14, 2016 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

===Los Angeles Lakers (2016–2017)===
On June 22, 2016, Calderón was traded, along with [[Jerian Grant]] and [[Robin Lopez]], to the [[Chicago Bulls]] in exchange for [[Derrick Rose]], [[Justin Holiday]] and a 2017 second-round draft pick.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nba.com/bulls/news/release/bulls-send-derrick-rose-new-york-five-player-deal |title=BULLS SEND DERRICK ROSE TO NEW YORK IN FIVE-PLAYER DEAL |work=NBA.com |date=June 22, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160624111247/http://www.nba.com/bulls/news/release/bulls-send-derrick-rose-new-york-five-player-deal/ |archive-date=June 24, 2016 |url-status=live }}</ref> On July 7, he was traded again, this time with two future second-round picks to the [[Los Angeles Lakers]] in exchange for the rights to [[Ater Majok]].<ref>[http://www.nba.com/lakers/releases/160707calderonacquired Lakers Acquire Jose Calderon]</ref> On December 5, 2016, he was ruled out for two to four weeks with a right hamstring strain.<ref>[http://www.nba.com/lakers/releases/161205-jose-calderon-hamstring-strain Jose Calderon Injury Update]</ref> On February 27, 2017, he was waived by the Lakers.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nba.com/lakers/releases/170227-calderon-waived|title=Lakers Waive José Calderón|work=NBA.com|date=February 27, 2017|access-date=February 27, 2017}}</ref>

After being waived by the Lakers, Calderón was set to join the [[Golden State Warriors]]. However, after [[Kevin Durant]] suffered a possible season-ending knee injury, the Warriors felt they needed a forward instead of a point guard to fill Durant's role.<ref>{{cite web|last=Slater|first=Anthony|url=http://www.mercurynews.com/2017/02/28/report-warriors-to-add-matt-barnes-not-jose-calderon-in-wake-of-kevin-durant-injury/|title=Warriors to add Matt Barnes, not Jose Calderon, in wake of Kevin Durant injury|work=MercuryNews.com|date=February 28, 2017|access-date=March 1, 2017}}</ref> The Warriors still honored their agreement with Calderón, signing him on March 1, 2017<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nba.com/warriors/news/calderon-signed-20170301|title=Warriors Sign José Calderón|work=NBA.com|date=March 1, 2017|access-date=March 1, 2017}}</ref> before waiving him later that day.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nba.com/warriors/news/calderon-waived-20170301|title=Warriors Waive José Calderón|work=NBA.com|date=March 1, 2017|access-date=March 1, 2017}}</ref> While he was only with the team for two hours, the Warriors agreed to pay Calderón the $415,000 he would have earned if he had been on the team for the rest of the season.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.msn.com/en-us/sports/nba/jose-calderon-will-earn-dollar415000-for-spending-two-hours-with-the-warriors/ar-AAnJ4xK|title=Jose Calderon will earn $415,000 for spending two hours with the Warriors|work=MSN.com|date=March 2, 2017|access-date=March 3, 2017}}</ref>

===Atlanta Hawks (2017)===
On March 4, 2017, Calderón was claimed off waivers by the [[Atlanta Hawks]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nba.com/hawks/atlanta-hawks-claim-jose-calderon-waivers|title=Atlanta Hawks Claim Jose Calderon Off Waivers|work=NBA.com|date=March 4, 2017|access-date=March 4, 2017}}</ref>

===Cleveland Cavaliers (2017–2018)===
[[File:CalderonCavs.jpg|thumb|Calderón with the Cavaliers in 2017]]
On July 10, 2017, Calderón signed with the [[Cleveland Cavaliers]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nba.com/cavaliers/releases/calderon-signing-2017|title=Cavaliers Sign José Calderón|work=NBA.com|date=July 10, 2017|access-date=July 10, 2017}}</ref> On April 3, 2018, Calderón scored a season-high 19 points, and recorded 4 assists and 4 rebounds, during a 112–106 win over the [[Toronto Raptors]].<ref>[https://www.wkyc.com/article/sports/nba/cavaliers/jose-calderon-continues-to-shine-for-cleveland-cavaliers/95-535096659 Jose Calderon continues to shine for Cleveland Cavaliers]</ref>

The Cavaliers eventually made it to the [[2018 NBA Finals]], before losing 4–0 to the [[Golden State Warriors]].

===Return to Detroit (2018–2019)===
On July 7, 2018, Calderón signed with the [[Detroit Pistons]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nba.com/article/2018/07/07/pistons-sign-jose-calderon-glenn-robinson-iii|title=Detroit Pistons sign free agents Jose Calderon, Glen Robinson III|work=NBA.com|date=July 7, 2018|access-date=July 7, 2018}}</ref>

==Executive career==
On November 4, 2019, the [[National Basketball Players Association]] (NBPA) announced that Calderón had joined them as the special assistant to the executive director for the [[2019–20 NBA season]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://nbpa.com/news/jose-calderon-joins-the-nbpa-as-the-special-assistant-to-the-executive-director|title=JOSÉ CALDERÓN JOINS THE NBPA AS THE SPECIAL ASSISTANT TO THE EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR|date=November 4, 2019|website=nbpa.com|access-date=November 4, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|first=Ben|last=Golliver|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/sports/2019/11/04/jose-calderon-played-pro-basketball-years-now-daunting-challenge-his-first-to-/|title=Jose Calderon played pro basketball for 20 years. Now, a daunting challenge: His first 9-to-5.|date=November 4, 2019|website=washingtonpost.com|access-date=November 4, 2019}}</ref>

On January 14, 2022, Calderón was hired by the [[Cleveland Cavaliers]] as a special advisor to the front office.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Cavaliers Hire Jose Calderon as Special Advisor to the Front Office|url=https://www.nba.com/cavaliers/releases/jose-calderon-hiring-220114|access-date=January 14, 2022|website=NBA}}</ref>

==National team career==
[[File:Spain national basketball team 2011 02.jpg|thumb|Calderón (second from left) with Spain at [[Eurobasket 2011]], which Spain won]]
Calderón enjoyed a successful international career. When he played for Spain's national youth team, he won the gold medal at the [[1998 FIBA Europe Under-18 Championship]], as well as the bronze medal at the [[2000 FIBA Europe Under-20 Championship]].<ref name=bio/> As his career advanced, the point guard began appearing for the [[Spain national basketball team|Spain national team]]. Calderón was part of the Spain national team which finished fifth at the [[2002 FIBA World Championship]] and second at the [[2003 EuroBasket]].<ref name=bio/> He was later named the team [[captain (sports)|captain]] during the [[Basketball at the 2004 Summer Olympics – Men's tournament|2004 Summer Olympics]],<ref name=bio/> and on September 3, 2006, Calderón and his Spanish squad defeated [[Greece national basketball team|Greece]], and won the [[2006 FIBA World Championship]], along with future Raptors teammate, [[Jorge Garbajosa]].<ref name=bio/> In [[2007 EuroBasket]], Calderón and Garbajosa again featured for the Spanish team. In the knockout stages, Calderón recorded a game-high 17 points as Spain defeated [[Germany national basketball team|Germany]] 83–55 in the quarterfinals.<ref>[http://www.eurobasket2007.org/en/cid_BqypGFJPHy-Nw4lwIAq,v3.gameID_5172-43-A-1.compID_qMRZdYCZI6EoANOrUf9le2.season_2007.roundID_5172.teamID_.html Game card] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070915110338/http://www.eurobasket2007.org/en/cid_BqypGFJPHy-Nw4lwIAq%2Cv3.gameID_5172-43-A-1.compID_qMRZdYCZI6EoANOrUf9le2.season_2007.roundID_5172.teamID_.html |date=September 15, 2007 }}, eurobasket2007.org, September 13, 2007, Retrieved September 14, 2007.</ref> In the semifinals, Calderón scored 18 points in Spain's defeat of [[Greece national basketball team|Greece]].<ref>[http://www.eurobasket2007.org/en/cid_BqypGFJPHy-Nw4lwIAq,v3.pageID_MIo9t,r8GfgCzft4jY3M,2.compID_qMRZdYCZI6EoANOrUf9le2.season_2007.roundID_5169.teamID_.gameID_5174-49-A-1.html Boxscore] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071022035123/http://www.eurobasket2007.org/en/cid_BqypGFJPHy-Nw4lwIAq%2Cv3.pageID_MIo9t%2Cr8GfgCzft4jY3M%2C2.compID_qMRZdYCZI6EoANOrUf9le2.season_2007.roundID_5169.teamID_.gameID_5174-49-A-1.html |date=October 22, 2007 }}, eurobasket2007.org, September 15, 2007, Retrieved September 16, 2007.</ref> The Spaniard led his team in points again in the final, but Spain lost 60–59 to [[Russia national basketball team|Russia]].<ref>[http://www.eurobasket2007.org/en/cid_BqypGFJPHy-Nw4lwIAq,v3.compID_qMRZdYCZI6EoANOrUf9le2.season_2007.gameID_5178-54-A-1.html Game card] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071221191602/http://www.eurobasket2007.org/en/cid_BqypGFJPHy-Nw4lwIAq%2Cv3.compID_qMRZdYCZI6EoANOrUf9le2.season_2007.gameID_5178-54-A-1.html |date=December 21, 2007 }}, eurobasket2007.org, September 16, 2007, Retrieved September 17, 2007.</ref> In the [[Basketball at the 2008 Summer Olympics – Men's tournament|2008 Summer Olympics]], Calderón was part of the squad that won the silver medal, but was injured and therefore could not play in the semifinal and the final games. He averaged 7.3 points per game in the tournament.<ref>[http://www.nbcolympics.com/basketball/statistics/gender=M/teamstats/rsc=BKM000000.html Statistics] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160604090837/http://www.nbcolympics.com/basketball/statistics/gender=M/teamstats/rsc=BKM000000.html |date=June 4, 2016 }}, nbcolympics.com, Retrieved August 26, 2008.</ref> Calderón won another silver medal at the [[Basketball at the 2012 Summer Olympics – Men's tournament|2012 Summer Olympics]].  He also made Spain's squad for the [[Basketball at the 2016 Summer Olympics – Men's tournament|2016 Summer Olympics]], but played only sparingly.

At the end of the Olympics, he announced his retirement from the national team.<ref>{{cite news|last1=de la Torre Sanz|first1=Manuel|title=Calderón deja la Selección: 'Es el sitio y el momento'|url=https://as.com/baloncesto/2016/08/30/mas_baloncesto/1472552269_287942.html|access-date=September 8, 2017|work=Diario As|date=August 30, 2016|language=es}}</ref>

==Player profile==
Calderón is one of the best [[free throw]] shooters in the NBA, having set an NBA record for free throw shooting (98.1%) in 2008–09 with 151 of 154 attempts. Known as a highly efficient player, Calderon's 2007–08 season would have made him [[50–40–90 club|one of eight players in NBA history]] to shoot at or over 50% on [[Field goal (basketball)|field goals]], 40% on [[three-point field goals]], and 90% on [[free throws]] for a full season, but he did not have enough free throws made to qualify.

